WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.123 --> 00:00:01.410
- Yes, dad. I know,

00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:04.200
but the mortgage rates
are crazy right now.

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:06.630
And the shampoo I use
for my luscious new locks

00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:08.880
has gone up a whole dollar.

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:10.140
What do you mean who cares?

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:11.670
Ugh, I gotta go.

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:12.840
- What was that about?

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:14.520
- I was just on the phone with my dad.

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:16.080
I'm trying to find some economic sympathy.

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:17.640
He said, well, back in my day,

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:20.160
I had to pay 8000% on my mortgage.

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:22.560
Doesn't he see that lots of
terrible things are happening?

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:25.050
- Well, he lived through
a pretty tough economy

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:26.760
between the 70s and 80s.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:29.220
High inflation, supply chain issues,

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:32.400
tension with the Soviet
Union, shocking gas prices

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:35.340
and ballooning interest rates,
which would eventually lead

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:37.440
to the worst downturn
since the Great Depression.

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:38.490
- So I am right.

00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:39.630
We should be freaking out.

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:41.070
- Hold on there, Henny Penny.

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:43.620
The sky is not necessarily falling.

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:45.180
There's a lot that's different,

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:46.890
but to be fair,

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:49.723
getting out an umbrella
might not be the worst idea.

00:00:49.723 --> 00:00:52.306
(upbeat music)

00:00:58.459 --> 00:01:01.350
- It's 1965, London, England.

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:03.150
The right honorable gentleman

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:05.430
Iain Norman Macleod is giving a speech

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:06.750
to the House of Commons.

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:08.280
In it, he would introduce

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:11.463
what would become famous
economic portmanteau.

00:01:13.027 --> 00:01:15.547
"We now have the worst of both worlds,

00:01:15.547 --> 00:01:17.587
"not just inflation on the one side

00:01:17.587 --> 00:01:21.127
"or stagnation on the other,
but both of them together.

00:01:21.127 --> 00:01:24.127
"We have a sort of stagflation situation,

00:01:24.127 --> 00:01:28.200
"and history in modern
terms is indeed being made."

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:30.300
- Stagflation as it came to be known

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:34.255
is when an economy experiences
a nasty blend of two things:

00:01:34.255 --> 00:01:37.290
inflation, the overall raising of prices

00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:39.420
and economic stagnation

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:42.210
usually indicated by
high unemployment rate

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:44.580
and decreased economic growth.

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:47.550
Now, at the time, this
combo was considered to be

00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:49.320
an economic Yeti.

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:51.330
Prevailing economic theory at the time

00:01:51.330 --> 00:01:54.480
was dominated by the work
of John Maynard Keynes.

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:56.280
After watching the devastation

00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:58.680
caused by the Great Depression, he argued

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:01.380
that governments needed
to get more involved

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:03.120
during economic downturns

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:06.600
in order to effectively cushion
the blow for its citizens.

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:09.420
This would in turn helped
stimulate the economy

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:10.560
back to health.

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:12.870
- Keynesians generally saw inflation

00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:14.850
as a result of economic growth

00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:16.740
and believed it to be directly correlated

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:18.480
to lower unemployment.

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:20.700
There's even a fancy graph to show this

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:23.370
called the Phillips Curve, a fabulous name

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:24.840
if I may say so myself.

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:26.790
However, the events of the mid 70s

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:29.572
would seemingly break this
somewhat reassuring pattern.

00:02:29.572 --> 00:02:32.460
Many are worried that economic indicators

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:35.880
in this post-COVID shocked
world are neatly aligned

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:39.240
for a similar meltdown that
we saw in the early 80s,

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:41.940
a time before most of the
people watching this video

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:43.110
were even born.

00:02:43.110 --> 00:02:46.170
- It's true that there are a
good amount of similarities.

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:50.490
In October of 1973, war
broke out between Israel

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:52.380
and a group of Arab countries.

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:53.772
The US and the Soviet Union

00:02:53.772 --> 00:02:56.520
tried to bolster their respective allies,

00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:59.520
which led to a rather
terrifying buildup of tensions

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:01.830
between two nuclear powers.

00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:02.915
Sound familiar?

00:03:02.915 --> 00:03:06.390
In response to the US's
support of the Israelis,

00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:09.900
the Organization of Arab
Petroleum Exporting Countries

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:11.940
proclaimed an oil embargo.

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:15.960
Within three months, the price
of oil nearly quadrupled.

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:18.570
- However, COVID and the
resulting energy hikes

00:03:18.570 --> 00:03:21.330
hit a really strong and growing economy.

00:03:21.330 --> 00:03:23.490
This wasn't the case in the 70s.

00:03:23.490 --> 00:03:27.210
The oil crisis poured salt
on an already bleeding wound

00:03:27.210 --> 00:03:28.350
of a bear market.

00:03:28.350 --> 00:03:32.280
In 1971, President Richard
Nixon clumsily broke away

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:33.930
from the Bretton Woods Agreement,

00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:36.860
an international monetary
system put in place

00:03:36.860 --> 00:03:40.710
in the summer of 1944,
and the dollar tanked.

00:03:40.710 --> 00:03:43.170
The toxic mix of events
would cause the market

00:03:43.170 --> 00:03:45.990
to lose around 48% of its value.

00:03:45.990 --> 00:03:48.540
It would take another
21 months to recover.

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:51.510
This became known as the Nixon Shock.

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:54.660
- Today, the war in Ukraine
is creating a similar effect

00:03:54.660 --> 00:03:59.010
of nudging elevated post-COVID
energy prices even higher,

00:03:59.010 --> 00:04:02.490
with European nations really
bearing the brunt of the hit.

00:04:02.490 --> 00:04:05.910
These energy costs shocks
are also coming on the heels

00:04:05.910 --> 00:04:09.030
of historically low interest
rates and rising debt loads.

00:04:09.030 --> 00:04:11.160
The same was true in the 70s.

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:12.930
However, it's important to point out

00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:16.255
that the magnitude of the price
shocks, at least as of now

00:04:16.255 --> 00:04:18.490
have not been nearly as drastic.

00:04:18.490 --> 00:04:21.451
Things did improve
following the crash of 1973

00:04:21.451 --> 00:04:25.770
as they did post 2020,
but not for that long.

00:04:25.770 --> 00:04:29.220
- The Iranian revolution kick
started another oil crisis

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:33.180
in 1979, more than doubling
the price in one year.

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:35.160
Unemployment was also on the rise,

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:37.980
and inflation was hovering around 7%.

00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:39.810
By this point, people were tired,

00:04:39.810 --> 00:04:43.200
and the economic ground was
ripe for a new approach.

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:46.560
Enter Paul Volcker as
the Chairman of the Fed.

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:49.230
Volcker was pretty convinced
the issue of inflation

00:04:49.230 --> 00:04:51.990
had been caused by the
government pumping money

00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:53.460
into the system.

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:56.910
By raising rates and making
money more expensive to borrow,

00:04:56.910 --> 00:05:00.870
he hoped demand would fall
and ease pressure on supply,

00:05:00.870 --> 00:05:02.940
hopefully resulting in stabilization

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:05.250
and eventually reduction of prices.

00:05:05.250 --> 00:05:08.070
So down came the interest rate hammer.

00:05:08.070 --> 00:05:11.340
By 1981, the average rate
on a 30-year mortgage

00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:15.660
was an eye watering 18.45%.

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:16.493
Yikes!

00:05:16.493 --> 00:05:19.050
No wonder I couldn't get
sympathy from my dad.

00:05:19.050 --> 00:05:21.090
- In some ways it did the trick.

00:05:21.090 --> 00:05:24.150
Inflation plummeted by the end of 1982,

00:05:24.150 --> 00:05:27.510
but that victory came at a huge cost.

00:05:27.510 --> 00:05:32.190
Unemployment at the time hit
an overall peak of 10.8%,

00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:34.440
but 90% of the unemployed

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:36.870
were in blue collar goods production

00:05:36.870 --> 00:05:39.300
like manufacturing and construction.

00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:43.350
This left a lasting impact on
large swaths of the country,

00:05:43.350 --> 00:05:45.120
now known as the Rust Belt

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:47.580
that heavily relied on these industries.

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:49.740
Entire communities were routed,

00:05:49.740 --> 00:05:51.810
and many have never recovered.

00:05:51.810 --> 00:05:55.020
There's still a lot of
debate as to whether the cure

00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:57.180
was worse than the disease.

00:05:57.180 --> 00:06:00.429
- Now it seems the Fed has decided to take

00:06:00.429 --> 00:06:02.700
a somewhat similar approach
and is raising rates.

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:04.920
Their hope is that they
can effectively walk

00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:06.450
an economic tightrope,

00:06:06.450 --> 00:06:09.390
raising rates while keeping
unemployment in check.

00:06:09.390 --> 00:06:12.300
Thankfully, inflation has
started to drift downward

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:15.360
since its peak of around
9% this past summer.

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:16.620
And while that sounds good,

00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:18.810
it's not all rainbows and unicorns.

00:06:18.810 --> 00:06:21.900
Think about what this
represents in real life terms.

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:23.580
Many people who had dreams

00:06:23.580 --> 00:06:26.580
of finally taking their
hard-earned savings to buy a home

00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:28.830
or start a business or grow their family

00:06:28.830 --> 00:06:31.796
are deciding that the
cost just isn't worth it.

00:06:31.796 --> 00:06:33.839
Individuals and communities feel this

00:06:33.839 --> 00:06:38.280
in a way that can't be captured
in some economic chart.

00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:41.659
- So is a terrible
recession on the way or not?

00:06:41.659 --> 00:06:43.800
No one really knows.

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:46.140
It is true that many of the ingredients

00:06:46.140 --> 00:06:49.440
going into this economic
recipe look the same,

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:53.070
but the oven they're getting
cooked in is very different.

00:06:53.070 --> 00:06:56.040
In general, our global
economy is far more flexible

00:06:56.040 --> 00:06:57.420
than it was back then.

00:06:57.420 --> 00:07:00.150
Most central banks have
mandates for things

00:07:00.150 --> 00:07:02.760
like price stability
and inflationary targets

00:07:02.760 --> 00:07:05.730
and pay super close
attention to this stuff.

00:07:05.730 --> 00:07:07.590
Even though COVID was a shock,

00:07:07.590 --> 00:07:10.320
the inflation that came
after really wasn't.

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:11.520
Unlike in the 70s,

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:13.980
we actually had historic
precedent for this,

00:07:13.980 --> 00:07:16.650
and most governments
were somewhat prepared.

00:07:16.650 --> 00:07:20.250
It also helps that unemployment
is currently really low.

00:07:20.250 --> 00:07:21.510
In fact, as of filming,

00:07:21.510 --> 00:07:26.040
it's only 0.2% higher than
it was in February of 2020.

00:07:26.040 --> 00:07:30.300
- However, we are in the midst
of a big economic transition.

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:33.510
COVID made the economy sick,
so we pumped it with meds

00:07:33.510 --> 00:07:35.829
that caused other problems
that we're now dealing with

00:07:35.829 --> 00:07:38.400
as we globally continue to heal.

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:41.550
- The best thing you can do
to prepare for times like this

00:07:41.550 --> 00:07:45.090
is to make sure you've got
a proper emergency fund

00:07:45.090 --> 00:07:47.520
and that your investments are diversified.

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:49.710
There's a reason that
professionals like us

00:07:49.710 --> 00:07:53.280
sound like a broken record about
this during the good times.

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:56.040
The deeper and wider your financial roots,

00:07:56.040 --> 00:07:57.900
the less likely you are to fall

00:07:57.900 --> 00:08:01.080
when the wind inevitably
changes direction.

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:02.688
- [Both] And that's our two cents

